[b]Speculative Markets 1.2.0[/b]

[list]
[*]Interest groups support roughly two traded goods and may oppose one. Their approval changes with booms, busts and interventions in those goods. Powerful groups react twice as strongly. Marginalized groups have no effect. A new leader may change one of a group's stances. See each stance on the interest group panel or Speculation tab.
[*]The new Monetary Standard law group offers Bimetallism, the Gold Standard and Fiat Money. Bimetallism makes rescues cheaper but boom endings harder. Gold cuts loan interest and trust losses but lengthens panics. Fiat makes rescues cheaper and panics shorter while feeding speculation. Laissez-Faire strengthens gold and Interventionism strengthens fiat. Adopting Command Economy on gold switches the currency to fiat.
[*]Financial Integration, a new power bloc principle group with three levels. It needs Sphere of Influence. A panic in one member costs the others 25% less Market Confidence. From the second level, members' Lending Limit rises by half the months by which the leader's exceeds theirs. At the third, the leader's Exchange counts its members' capital twice, up to its own.
[*]Investors may favor or shun a good after six months of prices 10% or more above or below its base price. A term lasts a year and fades over its last six months. A Favored good gains Investor Interest and a Shunned one loses it, from 10% and more the further its price is from base and the larger its share of the market. High Market Confidence strengthens Favor and softens Shunning. The good's makers get half of the effect as throughput. Longer price runs and larger goods are more likely to qualify, and each running term makes new ones less likely. A gold or red disc under a good's icon marks a term. Hover over it for the months left.
[*]Each good belongs to one of nine market sectors. If its sector was flat or moving the other way last month, a price swing can have as little as half its usual effect on Investor Interest. Sector trends weight traded goods by turnover. The price trend tooltip shows the previous sector trend and how much it dampened the good.
[*]The goods table groups traded goods under collapsible sector headings. Hover over a heading for its share of goods for sale and its previous price trend. A chart shows each sector's share of goods offered for sale, valued at base price. Hover over a sector to see its goods.
[*]The Speculation tab has a new Effects on Our Economy section. It shows what the market adds to or takes from weekly investment, yearly GDP, construction and trade. Hover over each figure to see where it comes from.
[*]Market Confidence shows what to expect next month. Its bar fills from the left in the map mode's colors, with a Risk marker where it would land if every current risk struck. Hover over the panic zone to see the Panic Threshold.
[*]Market Confidence appears beside GDP on the top bar. Hover for monthly changes and three years of history, or click to open Speculation.
[*]1.2 overrides the top bar, alert row and interest group panel. Other top bar mods such as The Great Rework need a merge.
[*]Clicking the Panic Risk alert opens the Finance window on the Speculation tab, and the Speculative Excess alert on the Banking tab (both used to open the journal entry).
[*]The Speculation goods list has a Risk column, tier icons and a Trend column. Sort headers switch off with another click. Filters pick a sector, an interest level, Favored or Shunned goods, or goods an interest group supports or opposes. The list also lags less.
[*]Domestic Market News now groups feverish speculation, boom endings and interest group stance changes by market sector in one bulletin. Routine news waits for three monthly reviews. A material bust or collapse brings it forward. The Individual Notices game rule restores per-good reports. Exchange contests still name each opponent separately.
[*]The Trading Floor shows the last monthly change beside Trust. Hover for the points gained or lost to boom endings, panics, lending standards and Bad Debts.
[*]Finance tooltips show how banking and trust figures are calculated. Contest tooltips include standing, push sizes and dates. Price trends show one decimal place.
[*]Settling a contest frees its slot. Each win or loss has its own notice naming the opposing Exchange, including when several contests settle in the same month. The competitor picker colors Exchanges by Standing.
[*]In observer mode, the Exchanges tab lists the observed country's competitors and the Exchanges contesting it.
[*]Paths to Renown rank Exchanges pursuing the speciality. Rankings refresh monthly, ties favor the holder and a new leader takes over immediately. The former +25% participation bonus is gone. AI Exchanges consider a stronger speciality when a slot unlocks.
[*]Price Setter weights market trade by the country's share of market GDP. Safe Harbor shows trust as a percentage. Rankings show the 10% deduction for each other speciality.
[*]AI Exchanges with free slots name favorable competitors immediately. Otherwise each has a 16% chance to choose one on its quarterly review.
[*]Contests settle five years after they start. Ending one early and naming the same rival starts a fresh contest.
[*]Paths to Renown and the blue chip count update every month. Another country's market shows its own blue chip count instead of 0.
[*]Panic Risk includes protection from World Centres, Blue Chips and power blocs. War Risk weighs both sides' forces. Booms at Risk updates immediately and the confidence breakdown retains each one-off change.
[/list]

[b]Balance[/b]
[list]
[*]Market Confidence starts at 50 and returns toward 50 when left alone (was 55). Banks now lend into the investment pool above 50 (was 55).
[*]Each booming good adds up to 6 Market Confidence a month (was 0.85), with all goods together capped at +3 per month before multipliers. Confidence closes 20% of the gap to its resting level each month (was 4%). A month of boom endings takes at most 35 (was 40). Market Mood fades more slowly.
[*]A collapse after a Feverish boom costs a sixth more Market Confidence (7 per point of hype, was 6).
[*]Speculative Excess starts building at 80 confidence (was 75) and grows by how far confidence is above 80 (was 70), so 5 a month at 85 (was 15). High volatility still counts from 70. It cools 5% a month under Tight credit (was 2%). A burst is no longer capped at 40 confidence (45 on High volatility), and what confidence cannot cover becomes Bad Debts.
[*]Panics last about 3 months before other effects (was 6.5).
[*]Each month of leverage past 3 adds 10% to a panic's cut to private construction, so all of it goes at 8 months (was always half), and 10% to its length, up to 90% (was none). A bank rescue halves the cut and caps the remaining term at half its length, rounded up. The technology and reform caps still apply.
[*]A panic calls back about 53% of outstanding credit at Strict lending standards, 75% at Sound and 98% at Reckless (was always 50%), and the debt falls by the same share. What the pool cannot pay, the banks lose.
[*]Each whole month of leverage adds 3.5% building throughput, up to 14% at 4 months (was 2%, up to 24% at 12 months).
[*]Ruinous leverage raises the Panic Threshold by up to 50 (was 40). An economy with no financial depth has its Panic Threshold 14 lower (was 5), so it panics only after a deep fall or when leverage lifts the threshold. Buy Up Bad Loans clears 30% of Bad Debts (was half).
[*]Each point of Bad Debts costs 3.5% throughput (was 0.4%), with at most 20 points. Bad Debts start to shrink only once they are felt in full, so they reach the size a panic shows. The worst cut 70% throughput and 15% investment at their peak (was about 13% and 25%). Their impact builds by 2 points per month. Once fully felt, they clear by 0.47 points per month under Normal or Loose Credit and 1.5 under Tight Credit. A maximum-size loss clears in about 4½ years or 2 years respectively if no new loss arrives.
[*]Banks lend less the more Bad Debts they carry, and nothing at or above 11 at the Sound midpoint, 5.5 at the strictest and 16.5 at the most reckless (was nothing while any Bad Debts lasted).
[*]Banks start in the middle of Sound lending standards (was the cautious edge of Sound) and turn stricter after a boom's losses a third as fast. Lending Standards swing lending by 35% (was 25%). Confidence adds at most 15% of a month's pool income to lending (was 40%), and 0.95% of outstanding credit is repaid each month (was 2%), so leverage builds more slowly but lasts longer.
[*]In quiet months, Tight Credit brings lax banks back toward Sound. A Money Market charter reduces trust losses.
[*]Financial District workers' standard of living follows Market Confidence. It is 3 lower while investors are Shaken, 1 lower while Wary, 1 higher while Buoyant and 3 higher in a Mania (was 1 higher in a Mania only).
[*]Booms are full size from the start (were 70% before the finance technologies). Stock Exchange makes them 24.5% larger and Investment Banks 28% (was 36% and 43%). Central Banking cuts the cost of Lend to the Banks by 30% (was 33%).
[*]An intervention ties up at most 50 Authority per good. A favored good's boom or bust moves approval for 180 days. Distrusted Exchanges still pay higher loan interest. Their low Trust carries no additional Industrialist approval penalty.
[*]Loan interest cuts could add up past 100% and make loans free. Removed are the cuts from World Centres (10%), the top five Exchanges (2% to 10%), Trusted (5%), Sound Finance (5%), each blue chip (1%), and Steady, Buoyant and Mania markets (4%, 7% and 10%). Converted Debt cuts interest by 10% (was 20%) and ends once debt falls below 20% of the credit limit.
[*]Sound Lending gives 2% to 8% Shopkeepers' investment pool efficiency by place (was 1.5% to 6% cheaper loans). First place in Lender to Nations gives 10% leverage resistance (was 5% cheaper loans).
[*]Up to 8 blue chips count, each in full (was the first 5 in full and the next 5 at half). Each adds 0.4% to the Capitalists' investment pool contribution (was 0.6%).
[*]Switching to another Exchange charter costs 25% of trust (was 50%). The first charter is still free.
[*]Investor interest gives a good's makers 2%, 4% and 6% throughput at Keen, Eager and Feverish (was 3%, 5% and 8%).
[*]Encourage and Restrain now scale with Market Confidence instead of aiming at Keen and Calm. Encourage multiplies interest by 2.2 at 55 confidence, 2.9 at 85 and 1.3 at 15. Restrain divides it by 2.2 at 55, 1.8 at 85 and 5.4 at 15, never below Calm. A good's intervention can change once every 3 months, with 7 days to undo activation before groups grant approval (was any time).
[*]A Great Issue adds up to 25% to the Exchange score, in full only for a good with 3% or more of trade (was 25% for any good).
[*]An Exchange can run 1 contest at Provincial Standing, 2 at National, 4 at International and 6 at World Centre (was 1, 2, 3 and 4).
[*]Third place on a Path to Renown carries the unranked drawback. Safe Harbor cuts private construction by 4%, Lender to Nations cuts investment pool efficiency by 2%, Railway Mania increases construction worker deaths by 4%, Deep Pockets cuts tax collection by 2% and Open Door cuts leverage resistance by 4%.
[/list]

Britain starts on the Gold Standard and every other country on Bimetallism. On an existing save, interest group stances and monetary laws arrive at the next monthly update. Existing Bad Debts convert once to preserve their throughput penalty, up to the new maximum of 70%.
